WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.599
A first shift is a time to ask, not guess.

00:00:02.829 --> 00:00:07.249
Devin is asked to use a cleaning liquid beside
a storage area with poor ventilation.

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:11.228
There is no readable label, and the supervisor
is helping another worker.

00:00:11.458 --> 00:00:18.759
A coworker says, “It is probably the usual
cleaner.” That description is not enough to
identify the substance or its safe use.

00:00:18.989 --> 00:00:19.838
Pause the task.

00:00:20.068 --> 00:00:23.496
We will separate what Devin observed from what
still needs confirmation.

00:00:23.576 --> 00:00:26.210
A hazard is a source or condition that could
cause harm.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:32.075
Here, the unlabeled container creates
uncertainty; the ventilation condition may
affect exposure.

00:00:32.305 --> 00:00:36.888
Devin should not infer the chemical from its
colour, smell, or a coworker’s guess.

00:00:37.118 --> 00:00:44.248
The missing information includes the product
identity, its instructions, possible exposure
routes, and the site procedure.

00:00:44.478 --> 00:00:51.003
This is a case about a missing safety control,
not about diagnosing a chemical or deciding
whether a law was broken.

00:00:51.083 --> 00:01:01.591
Canadian workplace safety guidance describes
three worker rights: to know about hazards, to
participate in health and safety decisions,
and to refuse work that may endanger someone.

00:01:01.821 --> 00:01:07.762
Devin can request the product label, the
relevant safety information, instruction, and
supervision.

00:01:07.992 --> 00:01:11.518
Devin can report the concern and take part in
choosing a safer method.

00:01:11.748 --> 00:01:19.432
If the hazard remains, the worker follows the
applicable refusal process; it is not a dare
or an invitation to confront someone alone.

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:22.964
Details belong to the current Alberta process
and workplace.

00:01:23.044 --> 00:01:26.389
The safe immediate move is to stop before
handling the container.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:32.957
If it is safe, Devin should keep other people
away from the area and alert the supervisor or
designated trained person.

00:01:33.187 --> 00:01:39.441
Devin should not smell the liquid, mix it with
another cleaner, pour it down a drain, or
carry it to find a label.

00:01:39.671 --> 00:01:42.365
Those actions can create exposure or a
reaction.

00:01:42.595 --> 00:01:47.748
If there is immediate serious danger, move to
safety and follow the site emergency
procedure.

00:01:47.828 --> 00:01:53.116
Before Devin acts: name the hazard, the
missing information, and the safest next step.

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.450
Pause here and decide.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:09.087
A strong response says: the container has no
readable label, ventilation is uncertain, and
Devin has not received the safe procedure.

00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:19.078
Devin pauses, keeps clear if safe, alerts the
supervisor, and asks for the identity, hazard
information, training, and approved control
before using it.

00:02:19.308 --> 00:02:24.546
The misconception is that a familiar-looking
product or coworker’s confidence makes
handling safe.

00:02:24.776 --> 00:02:25.435
It does not.

00:02:25.665 --> 00:02:29.588
After the hazard is addressed, Devin records
the outcome and follows up.

00:02:29.818 --> 00:02:37.030
For an actual workplace, check current Alberta
guidance and site instructions; this example
does not decide a legal case.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:43.593
Before closing, Devin checks who will identify
the liquid and what training is needed before
anyone resumes the task.

00:02:43.823 --> 00:02:50.044
If no trained person is available, the area
remains controlled and the concern is
escalated through the site process.

00:02:50.274 --> 00:02:59.634
The follow-up is not “clean it up later”; it
is to confirm that a responsible person has
identified the product, selected controls, and
explained how to work safely.

00:02:59.864 --> 00:03:04.960
This makes the handoff concrete and prevents
the unknown hazard from becoming the next
shift’s problem.

